```

This function takes an integer `n` as input and returns the nth Fibonacci number. The base cases are when `n` is 0 or 1, where the function simply returns that value. For larger values of `n`, the function uses a list `fib` to store previously computed Fibonacci numbers. It then iterates from 2 up to `n`, adding the previous two numbers in the list to get the current number in the sequence.

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), since it only requires a single pass through the list to compute each Fibonacci number.
